LOGBOOK.md:285:- AUTHORITY: The owner confirmed an authenticated Apple Developer session for Relux Works, LLC and authority for revision `2026-07-28.r12`: exactly the four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions on all four, and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered. No App Group record/capability, Keychain access-group portal mutation, iOS App ID, distribution profile, or `works.relux.proxy` change is authorized.
LOGBOOK.md:322:- SAFETY: The callback bridge checks terminal state after each suspended readiness/read/write boundary. A late socket completion can retire its tracked service but cannot mutate discarded buffers or report post-shutdown network progress.
LOGBOOK.md:335:- NONBLOCKING CLEANUP: A terminal socket enters an abort-drain mode that feeds EOF to receives and consumes only libssh2's already-retained outbound bytes. This lets the engine complete its nonblocking destructors without network I/O or fabricated operation success. Session destruction is bounded; failure remains explicit and keeps ownership visible rather than claiming a closed baseline.
LOGBOOK.md:416:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:484:- ROOT CAUSE (F2, and the more dangerous of the two): r5 corrected `TASK-260728-q5kjta` — the **human ceremony** — and left `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, the **unattended task that spends that authorization**, on the pre-r5 contract. Correcting an authorization node does not correct the node that consumes it. Its AC2 required the packet-tunnel entitlement "**only on provider identifiers**", which contradicted **r1**, not just r5: Apple requires `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has enforced it on all eight target rows since r2. Provider-only verification would have *passed* a portal state in which neither macOS host App ID carries the capability — surfacing at first build, after the sitting was over.
LOGBOOK.md:498:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
LOGBOOK.md:504:- FINDING: What the entitlement grants on macOS is readable, not arguable. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb` (26.5/25F71) contains exactly two `sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks for the key — lines **302-330** and **459-465** — conferring six grants. Three are **home-relative** and therefore break across the root/user split (group container r/w; `network-bind`/`network-outbound` inside it; `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>`). Three are **system-wide and do work across it**: `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` (**lines 326-328** — the XPC channel, and the only reason the macOS group ever looked load-bearing), and `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` (**line 329** — POSIX shm, which no candidate ever proposed). "App Groups do nothing on macOS" would be the tidy version and it is false; the verdict rests on *these grants are unused*, not on *the entitlement is inert*.
LOGBOOK.md:513:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 concluded "the app cannot reach a stopped provider" from the macOS 26.5 SDK header for `handleAppMessage:` — *"called by the framework when the container app sends a message to the provider"*. That sentence names **who sends**, not a liveness precondition, and the header for the sending side says nothing about launch at all. Apple's published API reference does: `sendProviderMessage(_:responseHandler:)` — *"If the extension is not running, **it should be launched to handle the message**."* One inference from a header that was silent, and revocation was declared permanently asynchronous, `379cpk` AC4 was declared unsatisfiable, and a 60-second startup budget was spent waiting for a seed. **Generalisable: for Network Extension lifecycle questions the shipped header and the published reference are not interchangeable — the header documents the signature, the reference documents the behaviour.** Check both before designing around an absence.
LOGBOOK.md:523:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
LOGBOOK.md:537:- DECISION: The row is attributed `project-architecture`, **not** `sandbox-consequence`, even though `com.apple.security.network.client` on the same target is a sandbox consequence and is equally Sparkle-driven. `network.client` follows mechanically from sandboxing alone; this key needs two independent project decisions to both hold (ADR-018's Sparkle choice **and** the host sandbox), so it is not derivable from "this target is sandboxed". Also added **rule X1**: the spec bans "unreviewed exception entitlement", so the contract now carries a reviewed-exception register plus an explicit banned set for Hardened Runtime relaxations — `get-task-allow` deliberately excluded from that set, since it is toolchain-injected on the development channel and governed by A10d instead. Without X1 this defect recurs one key at a time.
LOGBOOK.md:543:- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.
LOGBOOK.md:620:- FINDING: The new serial policy makes review Codex-owned and marks security, networking, DNS, parsers, signing, and release tasks `review=required`. The superseded `docs/spawn-policy.md` section it replaced recorded that Codex `gpt-5.6-sol` trips OpenAI's cybersecurity content filter on legitimate defensive security-testing work (fuzzing, hostile-input corpora, DNS/route leak tests, redaction tests) and exits non-zero mid-run.
LOGBOOK.md:866:- REWORK: Normalize thrown NetworkExtension start errors at the thin adapter boundary (or through an equivalently shared injectable translator), preserve unknown domain/code, cover both iOS/macOS seams plus the disabled-manager production path, and rerun focused normal/TSan, full core, formatting/diff/board checks, and both adapter builds before another reviewer cycle.
LOGBOOK.md:876:- REGRESSION: Both NetworkExtension adapters read `NEVPNConnection.status` before installing `NEVPNStatusDidChange`, so a terminal transition in that gap could be lost and an already-stopped session could falsely reach the 15-second `stopTimedOut` path.
LOGBOOK.md:882:- REGRESSION: Actor isolation did not serialize repository calls across suspended NetworkExtension callbacks, so concurrent operations could load and mutate overlapping manager generations. Both platform seams also used `NSNumber.intValue`, truncating `1.5` to current version 1 and overflowing `UInt64.max` into repairable corruption.
LOGBOOK.md:894:- DECISION: `ReluxTunnelCore` owns the serialized exact-predicate repository, immutable canonical configuration, 15-second callback tokens, stable errors, one-stale retry, and reload verification. The iOS/macOS adapter modules only translate public `NetworkExtension` objects and notifications into the injectable core seams.
LOGBOOK.md:1018:- DECISION: `relay/go.mod` NOT created — module scaffold stays with TASK-260715-27uz4n. `scripts/tests/test-relay-protocol-go.sh` compiles/vets/tests the package in a throwaway `.temp/` module (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, network-free). Workstation Go is 1.25.5; the pinned 1.26.5 is absent — module-pin verification remains with 27uz4n.
LOGBOOK.md:1153:- BLOCKER: The neutral network seam injects an `SSHTCPConnection` with readiness/read/write/close only, while `NIOSSHHandler` can run only inside a NIO `Channel`. The seam exposes neither a transferable connected socket nor an engine-bootstrap capability. Opening a second socket ignores injection; adapter-private downcast breaks fake replaceability; a socketpair pump is a forced-fit extra transport.
LOGBOOK.md:1277:- DECISION: iOS and macOS adapters share one locked `PacketFlowAdapterBoundary`; callback, task cancellation, and terminal adapter shutdown race for one continuation, while the callback registration remains a payload-free tombstone until NetworkExtension invokes it because `NEPacketTunnelFlow` exposes no deregistration API.
LOGBOOK.md:1305:- REVIEW REWORK 4: CR revision 2 rejected direct tag/selectable-ref release preparation because GitHub executes workflow code from the event-associated ref, letting a tagged workflow remove its own guard. The contract now makes tag push an unprivileged, output-free intake only and moves candidate acceptance to a protected-default-branch `workflow_run` that independently resolves run/ref/ancestry metadata, binds `github.workflow_sha` and a checked-in policy digest, ignores intake artifacts/caches/code/verdicts, and full-SHA-pins every credential-capable reusable workflow. Negative fixtures cover guard deletion in tagged code, non-default dispatch, workflow/policy mismatch, and candidate-ref/relative reusable calls before protected-environment access.
