--- /dev/null
+++ b/.temp/TASK-260715-whtdsf/research-github-release-request-rework5.md
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# TASK-260715-whtdsf — GitHub release-request trust evidence (rework 5)
+
+Checked: 2026-08-30
+
+Question resolved: Which GitHub Actions trigger loads release-preparation code
+from the protected default branch without allowing a caller-selected ref or
+candidate-controlled upstream workflow to define the acceptance guard?
+
+## Official platform evidence
+
+- `repository_dispatch` uses the last commit and ref of the default branch and
+  triggers only when the workflow file exists on the default branch.
+  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#repository_dispatch
+- Creating a repository dispatch event with a fine-grained token requires
+  repository `Contents: write`; it is therefore false to call the request
+  credential read-only. The payload is caller-controlled input.
+  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-dispatch-event
+- `GITHUB_TOKEN` permissions start from repository/organization defaults and
+  are then adjusted by workflow/job YAML. A restricted repository setting is a
+  default, not a hard ceiling for same-repository tag/push workflow code.
+  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax#how-permissions-are-calculated-for-a-workflow-job
+- GitHub-hosted runners have public Internet access by default. A table entry
+  saying `Network: None` is not an enforceable capability boundary on a normal
+  hosted runner.
+  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/concepts/runners/private-networking#about-github-hosted-runners-networking
+- Full commit SHA is the safest reference for reusable workflows; relative
+  workflow references resolve in the caller context and are not accepted for
+  credential-capable release authority.
+  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/reuse-automations/reuse-workflows
+
+## Binding conclusion
+
+Use one typed `repository_dispatch` to `release-prepare.yml`. Treat sender,
+request ID, version, and candidate SHA as untrusted. Independently require the
+expected GitHub App installation, protected-default-branch workflow SHA and
+policy digest, exact candidate ancestry, fresh nine-check evidence, and an
+absent intended tag. The short-lived dispatch App token is stored outside
+Actions, truthfully inventoried as `Contents: write`, and denied branch/tag
+ruleset bypass. Protected publication creates the tag later at the accepted
+candidate commit. Tag push, selectable-ref dispatch, and upstream
+candidate-controlled workflow completion are not release triggers.
+
+For PR jobs, record hosted-runner public egress as an actual capability. Their
+trust boundary is instead no production secrets, write token, protected
+environment, privileged runner, private-production route, or promotable output
+namespace. Credential-bearing release jobs require enforceable job-specific
+egress before secret access.
