+## Result
+
+Ready for exact-artifact independent agent review. Rework 5 closes CR revision
+3 without retaining the false claim that repository default permissions cap a
+same-repository tag workflow or that a standard GitHub-hosted runner has no
+network access.
+
+- `release-prepare.yml` has one typed default-branch `repository_dispatch`
+  trigger. Tag/push, selectable-ref dispatch, and candidate-controlled upstream
+  workflow completion are not release entry points.
+- The request payload and sender are untrusted. The first job independently
+  validates the expected GitHub App installation, unique request ID, candidate
+  ancestry, trusted workflow/policy digests, and absent intended tag.
+- The short-lived external dispatch credential is truthfully inventoried with
+  the GitHub API-required `Contents: write` scope and no protected branch/tag
+  bypass. It cannot accept a candidate, approve an environment, obtain
+  production secrets, create the protected release tag, sign, or publish.
+- Protected publication creates the intended tag atomically at the accepted
+  commit. Policy fixtures reject every `push.tags` workflow trigger.
+- Pull-request public egress is explicit runner capability, not a deny claim.
+  PR authority remains credential-free/read-only with no protected environment,
+  privileged runner, private-production route, or promotable namespace.
+  Credential-bearing release jobs require enforceable job-specific egress
+  before secret access.
+- Trust invariant 4 now distinguishes inventoried read-only metadata access from
+  write elevation after protected-environment approval.
+
+Gate A0, iOS/TestFlight/App Store workflows, workflow implementation,
+production credentials, and a Linux-runner requirement for the macOS prototype
+remain out of scope. Human governance ratification remains independently
+tracked by `TASK-260717-2d308k`; it blocks publication, not downstream
+implementation after agent acceptance.
